+
+
+Label and feature selection
+---------------------------
+Set labelCol on TrainClassifier to override the learner's label column.
+Otherwise, TrainClassifier inherits the learner's configured labelCol or its
+own declared default (for example, "label" for LogisticRegression). The resolved
+column must exist in the training data; an invalid wrapper label never falls
+back to the learner.
+
+By default, all training columns except the resolved label and the learner's
+nonempty weightCol are featurized. The weight remains in the training data for
+the learner. Use ignoredCols to additionally exclude identifiers, diagnostics,
+or other columns that should not be features. Its entries must name existing
+columns; repeated entries and redundant label/weight entries are harmless.
+There must be at least one remaining feature.
+
+Use inputCols instead to explicitly select the feature columns. inputCols and
+ignoredCols cannot both be set, including when ignoredCols is an empty list.
+Explicit inputCols is respected even when it intentionally includes a weight
+column. Clear one parameter before switching selection modes. ignoredCols has
+no default and is optional.
+
+featuresCol is the OUTPUT vector column name, not an input selector. It must
+not already exist in the input data. For example, use inputCols=["age"] to
+select an existing age column, and featuresCol="assembledFeatures" to name the
+new output vector. Featurize similarly uses inputCols and outputCol; it does
+not require a feature-column dictionary.
+
+Weighted PySpark example (with generated branch-built wrappers)::
+
+    from pyspark.ml.classification import LogisticRegression
+    from synapse.ml.stages import ClassBalancer
+    from synapse.ml.train import TrainClassifier
+
+    data = spark.createDataFrame(
+        [(0.0, -2.0, "a"), (0.0, 0.0, "b"), (1.0, 1.0, "c")],
+        ["outcome", "x", "tracking"],
+    )
+    weighted = ClassBalancer().setInputCol("outcome").fit(data).transform(data)
+    learner = LogisticRegression(
+        labelCol="outcome", weightCol="weight", maxIter=8, regParam=0.2
+    )
+    model = TrainClassifier().setModel(learner).setIgnoredCols(["tracking"]).fit(weighted)
+    predictions = model.transform(weighted.drop("outcome", "weight", "tracking"))
+
+Scoring uses the fitted feature pipeline, so automatically excluded or ignored
+training-only columns need not be present. Explicitly selected features remain
+required. A configured missing or invalid weight column still produces the
+underlying learner's error. For multiclass LogisticRegression, TrainClassifier
+uses OneVsRest and forwards its weight column.
+
+Compatibility when refitting
+----------------------------
+The automatic feature selection changes fitted results when previous training
+included weights as features. Column exclusions now use exact names, so a
+feature that is only a substring of the label name is retained. Inheriting the
+learner's default label is also supported when the wrapper label is unset.
+These changes take effect on new fits, including refits of loaded estimators.
+Old saved fitted models keep their original feature pipeline and may still
+require the weight column at scoring; loading does not migrate them.
+
+If labelCol and weightCol are the same column, the learner continues to receive
+the converted label as its weight (including reindexLabel's conversion when
+enabled). The automatic OneVsRest path uses a temporary copy only in its fit
+data to avoid duplicate column names; its persisted weightCol names that copy.
+The copy is not a feature and is not required for scoring. No label/weight
+column-distinctness restriction is introduced.
+
+TrainRegressor and AutoTrainer do not gain ignoredCols or these label-resolution
+changes. Other training-only learner columns such as validationIndicatorCol or
+initScoreCol are not automatically discovered; use ignoredCols or explicit
+inputCols when they should not be features.
